a  when i saw that he was coming on his own  here  i thought i can't sit at home and let  him protest  for human rights abuses that involve  people from my community andrew says  he's motivated  by history my wife's  grandparents were either gaster  auschwitz in the extermination camp  there or killed in the concentration  camp there  but never before except once in history  have millions of men  women and children being rounded up and  put in concentration camps  and we know how that ended and we said  never again  beijing says the camps are voluntary  education centers  used to counter islamic extremism it  denies allegations of torture  separating children from parents and  forced sterilization  don't listen to fake news don't listen  to fabrications mounting evidence though  triggered u.s  sanctions against china with calls for  countries like canada  to follow suit the reason that you know  many countries  are silenced and they're not taking any  action is again  like i repeatedly say it's china's money  roshan abbas's sister was put in a  detention center two years ago  she says what's happening is genocide a  term  not being used by western governments  anybody who's learnt history knows what  happened in the second world war  and there are so many similarities it  seems to be happening again  and we need to stop it andrew is  inviting others to  do something similar at chinese  embassies around the world  one evening at a time redmond shannon  global news  london the italian coast guard conducted  a rescue mission in the mediterranean  coming to the aid of a migrant rescue  vessel that was funded by the popular  street artist  banksy the boat which was carrying more  than 200 rescued migrants  put out a call for help saturday in a  series of tweets  after it became stranded at sea unable  to move due to overcrowding  eventually the italians and a ship from  a german charity arrived and transferred  the passengers to safety  banksy the secretive artist known for  his social commentary  funded the boat accusing european  authorities of ignoring distress calls  for migrants  at sea still ahead covet 19  and the classroom as students return to  school our team of medical experts is  taking your questions about parenting  in a pandemic  thousands of students in quebec returned  to the classroom last week  and many more will follow across the  country in the coming days and besides  notebooks and pencils  their school days may involve masks  cohorts  and a lot of added anxiety to help  students and parents prepare we are  joined now by dr  dina kulik a pediatrician and emergency  room physician in toronto  and dr shimi kang a psychiatrist based  in vancouver thank you both so much for  joining us  our first viewer question comes from  nicole  moza who asks do my children have to  stay home for two weeks  every time they get a cold or flu  symptoms to ensure it's not covid19  and do we as parents also have to miss  work for a couple of weeks so dr kulick  let's start with you  uh obviously the specific rules in that  regard depend on your exact school board  but what's your advice  i think the most conservative approach  would be to stay home for the next 14  days  anyone that's been exposed to that  person who has illness  the reason why for me is that we know  we're contagious for at least two weeks  once we're unwell and i think it's  impossible at the moment  to really know if your child's illness  or your illness is in fact coveted or  not because the test  is pretty imperfect i'm not sure it's  very reliable or accurate  in young people especially and so if you  have a negative test  i still wouldn't be making decisions  about return to school  based on that negative test instead i  think the conservative  right approach at the moment so that we  don't risk putting ourselves  in a second wave would be to stay home  for the next two weeks  right and dr kang over to you next any  advice for parents  on how to talk to their children about  anxieties they might have  when it comes to heading back to school  yes and there's definitely anxiety in  those kids who are going  in person but also remote learning  they're worried about being left behind  academically and social isolation those  going in person  of course have safety concerns so you  want to have age appropriate questions  in the sense and i always say start with  open  and cure being open and curious so  really just ask them what's on their  mind  they may have anxieties about all kinds  of things so that's a great place to  start  and then guide them towards some  practical problem solving some things  that you may foresee  whether it's wearing masks or staying  focused online  and then really build a community and  start asking them  and telling them that there are people  that they can also turn to within their  school environment so that they can  communicate their emotional concerns  all right thank you for that and don't  go anywhere we've got more questions for  you we got to take a quick break but  when we come back  students in some parts of the country  will be required to wear masks when they  return to school  but are face shields a viable option  we're answering more of your kovit 19  questions  after the break  welcome back since the start of the  pandemic global news has been taking  your questions about covid19  straight to the experts and we are  joined now by dr dina kulik who's a  pediatrician  and emergency room physician in toronto  and dr shimi kang  a psychiatrist based in vancouver thank  you both again for joining us  uh dr kulik let's go to you for this  next question that comes from wendy  mccarthy  particularly in a context where students  may be required to wear masks when they  return to the classroom  she's wondering if face shields will  provide adequate protection  we don't really know there hasn't been  any big studies that have shown that  they're as effective or less effective  but the idea behind a mask is that it  protects your sputum your mucus your  droplets from leaving your body  and going into the air to potentially  affect other people  so if the fat face mask goes very low or  the shield goes very low  theoretically you're a bit blocked but  what i've seen um in my practice and  even on the street is a lot of people  are wearing face shields that don't go  very low they might only go  to about here or they're you know moving  around etc so  we don't really know the answer whether  they're effective or not when your child  and you could  wear a mask that's what i would prefer  because it's closer to your face it's  going to  prevent more droplets from staying  towards you versus going out  into the air right good to know and  especially certainly a lot of parents  wondering if it's even realistic to  expect children young children to be  wearing masks for long periods of time  dr kang over to you for this next  question and especially  pertinent for students who will be doing  remote learning or parents who just  decide to keep their kids at home  how do you balance concerns for their  physical health and covid19  with concerns about the impact that  long-term isolation can have  on their mental health yeah so we're  certainly seeing  increased rates of loneliness we know  that in general even before the pandemic  anxiety social disconnection was an  issue  so really making it a priority  if you're on remote learning schedule it  into your day  social connection prefer technology that  has face and voice so we need to see  each other hear each other's voices  and have that uh really scheduled like  homework time  where you're connecting with people in a  meaningful way we want  social bonding going deeper than just  socializing  really important for the tween and teen  because their brains are  more sensitive to feelings of isolation  so really i know there's a lot to think  about right now  and academics is part of it but school  is very important to keep those social  connections  and make that a priority and dr kulick  last question to you and a quick one 30  seconds or less this is a common one  from grandparents who are now wondering  once their grandchildren return to  school  should they keep their distance i would  i would assume that if kids are going  back to school  they're not only being exposed to 15 or  20 or more kids in their group  they're exposed to their people that are  you know bringing their own illnesses  from their bubbles  they're the people that are higher risk  the grandparents so i would be trying to  keep the distance if you can  uh and that's a heartbreaker and  unfortunately we'll have to leave it  there dr dina kulik dr shimi kang  thank you both so much for joining us  thank you  and thank you for joining us that is  global national for this sunday i'm jeff  semple  tonight's your canada is campbell river  british columbia  if you'd like to share your canada with  us email your photos to viewers at  globalnational.com  robin gill will be back here with you  tomorrow have a great night  you
 

